Key differences in a table format through code:

```java
// FEATURE       | throw                          | throws
// ──────────────|────────────────────────────────|──────────────────────────
// WHAT          | Statement (throws exception)   | Declaration (warns callers)
// WHERE         | Inside method BODY             | In method SIGNATURE
// HOW MANY      | ONE exception per throw        | MULTIPLE: throws A, B, C
// FOLLOWED BY   | Exception OBJECT (new X())     | Exception CLASS NAMES
// REQUIRED FOR  | Actually raising exception      | Checked exceptions only
// COMPILE CHECK | None (can throw anywhere)       | Compiler forces on checked

// throw creates the OBJECT:
throw new IOException("File not found");         // new + throw = object created + thrown

// throws names the CLASSES:
public void read() throws IOException, SQLException { }  // just class names, no objects
```

Unchecked exceptions don't NEED throws (but can have it):

```java
// UNCHECKED: throws NOT required (but allowed for documentation):
public void withdraw(BigDecimal amount) {  // NO throws clause!
    throw new IllegalArgumentException("bad");  // unchecked → no throws needed!
}

// CHECKED: throws REQUIRED (or try-catch):
public void readFile() throws IOException {  // MUST declare throws!
    throw new IOException("fail");           // checked → throws required!
}

// You CAN add throws for unchecked (for documentation):
public void process() throws IllegalArgumentException {  // allowed but optional
    throw new IllegalArgumentException("bad");
}
```

